at first glance darvid olivera's artwork  looks almost like sketches drawn in air  but the sculptures are actually made of  wire suspended in space the portuguese  sculptor has already won several awards  he began experimenting with wire  straight out of art school I searched a  line that could go outside the paper I  was a big fan of drawing so I connected  sculpture and wire assumed the most  interesting material to be able to join  both his work is part of a group  exhibition on view at the carpe diem  Center for Contemporary Art in Lisbon  through December the historic palace in  the centre of the city  isn't just an exhibition space and for  Oliveira it was also a source of  inspiration and so is the Tagus River  which he calls an important source of  strength his apartment doubles as a  studio Oliveira does his research here  and designs for sculptures along with  wire he also uses cloth plastic and  small objects he makes his sculptures by  hand using simple tools he also wants to  use as few natural resources as possible  which is why he uses a minimum of  material to make a line that surrounds  the surface that's in my mind and I just  go for it it doesn't have doesn't have  to be a direct process but usually is I  don't go for the drawing in 2d  olivera's work has also been attracting  attention outside portugal in 2013 one  of his sculptures starred in a Japanese  marketing spot his art teeters between  sculpture and drawing that's also why it  was shown at the casa de cerca outside  Lisbon which is devoted only to drawing  he has a sculptures mind but sculpture  for him is something that he does with a  drawing process so he works with wire as  if he were drawing on on air this game  with perspective is something that  fascinates many artists one is French  artist Bell nappa whose work only comes  alive when viewed from the correct angle  that's also the idea behind German  Street artist Edgar Munez work who's  known for his 3d illusionist sidewalk  paintings Tim Noble and sue Webster  create works from scraps and rubbish to  cast shadow sculptures the sense of the  unexpected is part of the fascination I  think that's a way to get people  attention just for them to realize if  they look a bit closer and closer they  will get some other things today David  olivera's works sell for up to 6,000  euros a piece many have been exhibited  internationally above all he wants his  art to spark a dialogue I would like to  see my art in public spaces for it would  be accessible to everyone one of the  characteristics of this work was the  idea of children a public that is not as  used to art or an art space  in his current show David Olivera  focuses his delicate wire sketches on  creatures from the deep blue sea
 

